Title: The Messenger
Order: 1
Production Code: 1x01

Cast: JIM DAVIS, THE PRESIDENT, THE JUNIOR CLERK, THE VAULT CLERK, THE NARRATOR. Running time target: 12-15 minutes. Adapted from W.E.B. Du Bois, "The Comet" (Darkwater, 1920).

PRODUCTION NOTE: This is a full-cast audio drama. Every SFX, MUSIC and AMB cue below is the production's, not Du Bois's; spoken lines are the source's own words except the invented connective lines listed in imports/source-material.md. Period language is preserved as written.

INT. BLACK - NO SOUND

MUSIC: BED IN - a single low string note, barely above silence, sustained like a held breath

NARRATOR
(quiet, close to the microphone)
He stood a moment on the steps of the bank, watching the human river that swirled down Broadway.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. BROADWAY - THE STEPS OF THE BANK - MORNING

AMB: Broadway at full roar - horse hooves and motor horns, iron-rimmed wheels, a streetcar bell, a thousand shoes on pavement, newsboys calling

SFX: a streetcar passes, bell clanging, its wheels shrieking on a curve

The sound of the city is the star of this opening. Let it fill the headphones; this is the last time it will be this loud.

NARRATOR
Few noticed him. Few ever noticed him save in a way that stung. He was outside the world.

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(bitter, almost under his breath)
Nothing.

SFX: footsteps going by - brisk, none of them stopping, none of them slowing

Bits of overheard talk drift past in the walkers, spoken very close, then gone, as if on a passing breeze:

WALKER ONE (O.S.)
The comet?

WALKER TWO (O.S.)
The comet----

WALKER THREE (O.S.)
(laughing)
Did you see it last night?

AMB: the roar of the street under all of it, steady

SFX: the heavy brass door of the bank swings; the street drops to a hush behind it

INT. THE BANK - MAIN FLOOR - CONTINUOUS

AMB: marble room tone - high ceilings, a clock ticking, pens scratching, a typewriter somewhere

SFX: a heavy door swings and clicks shut, sealing out Broadway

The president enters at the same time as Jim, and smiles at him.

THE PRESIDENT
(genial, patronizing)
Well, Jim, are you scared?

JIM DAVIS
(shortly)
No.

THE JUNIOR CLERK
(affably, breaking in)
I thought we'd journeyed through the comet's tail once.

THE PRESIDENT
Oh, that was Halley's. This is a new comet, quite a stranger, they say - wonderful, wonderful! I saw it last night.

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - a parlor piano, faint, from a gramophone in a back office, something light and sentimental

THE PRESIDENT (CONT'D)
(turning to Jim)
Oh, by the way, Jim - I want you to go down into the lower vaults today.

SFX: Jim's step stops

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(dry)
Of course they wanted him to go down to the lower vaults. It was too dangerous for more valuable men.

THE NARRATOR
He smiled grimly and listened.

THE PRESIDENT
Everything of value has been moved out since the water began to seep in. But we miss two volumes of old records. Suppose you nose around down there - it isn't very pleasant, I suppose.

JIM DAVIS
(a beat too long)
Not very.

SFX: Jim's footsteps leave, steady and measured

MUSIC: SOURCE BED OUT

INT. THE BANK - THE VAULT CLERK'S CAGE - MOMENTS LATER

AMB: a quieter corner; a ticking clock, a faint hiss of a steam pipe

SFX: a ring of iron keys, heavy, handed over; they jingle once and settle in Jim's palm

THE VAULT CLERK
(friendly, offhand)
Well, Jim - the tail of the new comet hits us at noon this time.

Jim says nothing. He takes the keys.

SFX: his steps going down; an iron stair door opens on a hinge that needs oil

THE VAULT CLERK (O.S.)
(calling after him, cheerful)
Mind the damp!

SFX: the stair door closes behind him; the bank is gone

INT. THE STAIRS - DESCENT

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings and a single bowed bass, slow and descending; a scrape of cello like footsteps

Let this descent take as long as it needs. Build the depth in layers: first the muffled street through the floor, then nothing.

AMB: Broadway above - muffled, thick, the sound of feet through stone, "the dim light filtered through the feet of hurrying men"

NARRATOR
Down he went beneath Broadway, where the dim light filtered through the feet of hurrying men.

SFX: footsteps on stone stairs, echoing, going down

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Down to the dark basement beneath.

SFX: a second iron gate unlatched, creaking; keys

AMB: the street above fading to a low hum, then to nothing

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Down into the blackness and silence beneath that lowest cavern.

SFX: a third door; the lock complaints; the hush of an enormous space

INT. THE LOWER VAULTS - THE LOWEST CAVERN

AMB: dripping water, steady, from many places at once; a deep room tone, almost a pressure in the ears

SFX: the creak of a dark lantern's shutter opening; a small circle of light we only hear

NARRATOR
Here with his dark lantern he groped in the bowels of the earth, under the world.

SFX: boots in standing water - slow, wet

SFX: the last great iron door, thrown back; a vast grinding of rust on rust

JIM DAVIS
(a long breath out)
Here at last was peace.

NARRATOR
He drew a long breath as he threw back the last great iron door, and stepped into the fetid slime within.

SFX: a squelch of mud underfoot

SFX: a rat bolts past, claws scrabbling on stone, a splash

JIM DAVIS
(sharp intake of breath, then steadying)
Rat.

SFX: a soft brush of cobweb against the face; he wipes at it

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(moodily)
Shelf by shelf, along the mud. Crevice, corner. Nothing.

SFX: knuckles tapping stone, a hollow place, a solid place, a hollow place; he moves on

SFX: he sounds the far wall with a bit of metal - tink, tink, a different note

JIM DAVIS
(low, to himself)
Somehow the wall feels different.

SFX: pushing, prying; a grunt of effort; nothing

SFX: he turns away, steps - then stops. The steps come back.

NARRATOR
He started away. Then something brought him back.

SFX: heavy shoving, a strained exhale; a deep, hollow rumbling as though a hidden hinge is turning

MUSIC: STING - a single low, bowed note under the sound of stone swinging

SFX: the whole black wall swinging on mighty hinges; a huge slow grinding; cool air breathes out of the dark

INT. THE SECRET VAULT

AMB: stillness; dust, old air; not even the dripping now

JIM DAVIS
(hushed)
It's - a vault. Some hiding place of the old bank. Unknown in newer times.

SFX: his footsteps on dry stone - far enough from the water that the sound is cleaner, brittle

SFX: a long, narrow room: shelves along both sides; his lantern shutter clicks wider

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(finding them)
There. On the high shelf. The two missing volumes - and others.

SFX: heavy bound books lifted down, set carefully aside; a puff of dust

NARRATOR
At the far end, an old iron chest.

SFX: his steps to the end of the room, slow

JIM DAVIS
(touching the lock)
Old. Strong. Rusty.

SFX: fingers over a vast, old-fashioned lock; a rattle of loose iron

SFX: a bit of iron found, scraped over the stone floor; then the first bite of metal in the rusted hinge

NARRATOR
The rust had eaten a hundred years, and it had gone deep.

SFX: prying - slow, a creak, a pause, another; a patient effort

MUSIC: BED IN - low, patient strings, rising by half-steps; a held breath

SFX: the lid lifting, grinding, wearily; the old hinge groans

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Slowly, wearily, the old lid lifted, and with a last, low groan lay bare its treasure.

SFX: the groan, then a clink of metal - coin on coin

JIM DAVIS
(whispered)
The dull sheen of gold.

MUSIC: SWELL - a single bright chord, brief, a gleam of light on metal, instantly shut off

SFX: BOOM. A low, grinding, reverberating crash - a stone door the size of a house, closing, felt in the chest as much as heard

MUSIC: CUT - dead stop

AMB: absolute silence for four full seconds. Hold it.

SFX: dust sifting down; a pebble clicks; Jim's breath, ragged

JIM DAVIS
(low)
All was black and still.

SFX: he gropes for the lantern; knocks it against stone; it rolls; he catches it

SFX: the shutter clicks; light, the stone door sealed flat against the wall

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(realizing)
The door. The great stone door has swung to.

NARRATOR
He forgot the gold, and looked death squarely in the face.

A beat. Then, because it is who he is:

JIM DAVIS
(a sigh)
Well.

NARRATOR
Then with a sigh he went methodically to work.

SFX: pounding with the flat of a hand on stone; searching; a boot kicked against the base of the door

SFX: shoving with his whole weight, a long exhale; stone does not move

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(cold, controlled)
Searched. Pounded. Pushed. Worked.

MUSIC: BED IN - low drone, patient, with a slow pulse like a clock; it should feel like time passing without the audience being told

SFX: sweat drops on stone; his breath, getting heavier

NARRATOR
The cold sweat stood on his forehead. But he searched, and pounded, and pushed, and worked.

SFX: a small sound; a time-cut - the lantern wick sputters; a new match; the lantern relit

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Until, after what seemed endless hours -

SFX: his hand finds something; a scrape of metal on metal; a catch

SFX: a deep lock-clunk, then the great stone door grinding open - slowly, harshly, on its hinges

SFX: the door stops against something soft and heavy - a dull, wrong thud

MUSIC: STING - a low brass note, flat and mournful

JIM DAVIS
(a whisper)
I have just room to squeeze through.

SFX: cloth dragging through a narrow gap; a grunt

NARRATOR
There lay the body of the vault clerk, cold and stiff.

SFX: Jim's steps stop

AMB: the dripping water has changed - fewer drops, as if the room itself is holding something back

JIM DAVIS
(very low)
Oh, God.

SFX: his breath catching; swallowing hard, nauseated

NARRATOR
The air seemed unaccountably foul, with a strong, peculiar odor.

SFX: a step forward, a clutch of fingers at nothing; a heavy fall across a body

MUSIC: SWELL - dissonant, fading to nothing under the sound of a man falling

BLACK.

MUSIC: BED OUT
